 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Where is Spanish inheritance tax filed if the heir lives abroad?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "In general, with the Spanish Tax Agency. If the heir is not resident in Spain, or the deceased was not resident here, the tax is managed by the National Tax Management Office in Madrid, on form 650 as a self-assessment (Law 22/2009, article 32.2, and Law 29/1987, second additional provision). It can be filed online, without travelling, by the heir if there is only one, by an authorised representative or by a registered tax intermediary. The exception is the Basque Country and Navarre: if the deceased was resident there, or if the greatest value of what the non-resident heir inherits is located there, the tax belongs to their own foral tax authority (Law 12/2002, article 25, and Law 28/1990, article 31)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I apply a region's tax reliefs if I do not live in Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, if it suits you. A non-resident heir is entitled to apply the rules of the region where the deceased was resident; if the deceased was not resident in Spain either, those of the region where the greatest value of the estate's assets in Spain is (Law 29/1987, second additional provision, paragraph One). According to the Spanish Tax Agency, this also applies to residents outside the European Union. The tax is calculated under both sets of rules before filing."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is there a tax-free threshold for inheritance tax in Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no single tax-free amount for every heir: what reduces the tax is a reduction of the taxable base that depends on the heir's relationship with the deceased. Under the State rules, the main amounts are 15,956.87 euros for descendants (including adopted children) aged 21 or over, spouses and ascendants (including adopting parents); for descendants under 21, 15,956.87 euros plus 3,990.72 euros for each year under 21, up to 47,858.59 euros; 7,993.46 euros for brothers and sisters, nephews and nieces, uncles and aunts, and in-laws in the ascending or descending line; and none for more distant relatives or unrelated heirs (Law 29/1987, article 20.2.a). Under the Valencian rules, which a non-resident heir can apply when the deceased lived in the Valencian Community, the amount is 100,000 euros for descendants (including adopted children) aged 21 or over, spouses and ascendants (including adopting parents), and for descendants under 21, 100,000 euros plus 8,000 euros for each year under 21, up to 156,000 euros; the tax that remains for these relatives has a 99% relief on assets declared in full in a self-assessment filed on time, or filed late without a prior request from the tax authorities (Valencian Law 13/1997, articles 10.1.a and 12 bis). Each region sets its own figures, so the amount that matters depends on which rules apply to the inheritance."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the deadline for inheritance tax if I live abroad?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Six months from the date of death (Inheritance and Gift Tax Regulations, Royal Decree 1629/1991, article 67.1.a). An extension of another six months is only granted if requested within the first five months, and it carries late-payment interest (article 68). The municipal tax on the increase in value of urban land (plusvalía municipal) has its own six-month deadline, extendable up to one year, and that extension is requested from the town hall (Local Finance Act, article 110.2.b)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do I need a representative in Spain for the inheritance?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It depends on where you are resident. According to the Spanish Tax Agency, an heir resident neither in the European Union nor in a European Economic Area State with mutual assistance must appoint a representative resident in Spain for inheritance tax; an heir resident in the Union or in those States does not have to. For the inherited property, non-resident income tax has its own rule (Non-Resident Income Tax Act, article 10)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can you handle the inheritance without me travelling to Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "We can review the documents remotely and, if you ask us to file the tax return, act for you before the Spanish Tax Agency with the necessary power of attorney. To discuss your case in English, write to avanza@grupoavanzaconsultores.es."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does inheritance tax in Spain for non-residents cover assets outside Spain?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. An heir who is not resident in Spain is taxed in Spain only on assets and rights located in Spain, or that can be exercised or must be performed here, and on amounts from life insurance contracts taken out with Spanish insurers or entered into in Spain with foreign insurers operating here (Law 29/1987, article 7). Assets located abroad are not taxed in Spain for that heir; whether the country of residence taxes them depends on that country's law."}}]}</script>
