     <h2>After the inheritance: the property keeps being declared</h2>
     <p>If the inherited property is urban, is not used in a business and is not let, a non-resident heir has imputed income from it (Non-Resident Income Tax Act, texto refundido de la Ley del Impuesto sobre la Renta de no Residentes, article 13.1.h), which is declared on form 210; if it is let, the rental income is taxed instead, and the deductible expenses and filing deadlines depend on the owner's circumstances. Our guide to <a href="modelo-210-non-resident-tax-spain.html">form 210 for non-residents</a> explains who files it and when. For this tax, a non-resident who lives neither in the European Union nor in a European Economic Area State with mutual assistance must appoint a representative resident in Spain in the cases listed in article 10.1, which include the Tax Agency requiring one because of the amount or nature of the income or because the person owns a property in Spain. Separately, a resident of a country or territory with no effective exchange of tax information must appoint one if they own assets or rights in Spain, excluding securities traded on official secondary markets. Failing to do so is a serious infringement, with a fixed fine of 2,000 euros, or 6,000 euros in the second case (article 10.4). <a href="selling-property-spain-non-resident.html">If the property is sold later</a>, the buyer generally withholds 3% of the agreed price and pays it to the Tax Agency on account of the seller's tax, which is not the final tax; the general rate for capital gains is 19% (article 25, sections 1.f and 2).</p>
+    <p>If the deceased was also not resident in Spain and left Modelo 210 returns for the property unfiled, those outstanding tax obligations pass to the heirs, subject to the civil-law rules on accepting the inheritance, but penalties do not (<a href="https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2003-23186#a39">General Tax Act, article 39.1</a>). The Tax Agency can only assess what is not time-barred; the general limitation period is four years (<a href="https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2003-23186#a66">article 66</a>). How to bring them up to date is explained in <a href="regularising-taxes-spain-foreigner.html">overdue tax returns in Spain</a>.</p>
 
     <h2>If you receive a gift instead</h2>
     <p>A gift to someone who is not resident in Spain is also handled, in general, by the National Tax Management Office, on form 651, within thirty working days from the day after the gift (Inheritance and Gift Tax Regulations, article 67.1.b). If the gift is a property located in Spain, a non-resident recipient can apply the rules of the region where the property is; if movable assets located in Spain are given, such as money in a Spanish bank account, the rules of the region where they were located for the greatest number of days in the previous five years (Law 29/1987, second additional provision, paragraph One, letters c and e). If the gift is a property located in the Basque Country or Navarre, or the greatest value of what is given is located there, it belongs to their foral tax authority; if a single document gives assets that belong to different tax authorities, each authority charges the tax on the assets that belong to it, at the average rate for the whole gift (Law 12/2002, article 25, sections One, letters b and d, and Three; Law 28/1990, article 31, sections 1, letters b and c, and 2). Under the Civil Code, <a href="gift-of-money-from-abroad-spain.html">a gift of money</a> does not need a public deed: an oral gift requires simultaneous delivery, and otherwise it must be made in writing (article 632). Regional allowances and reliefs, however, can require a public document and other conditions that have to be met before signing.</p>
